### Page 1 of 10

```text
MEMORANDUM
TO:

The Honorable Mayor Patrick Collins and City of Cheyenne Council Members
FROM:

Robin Lockman, City Treasurer
DATE:

September 1, 2022
SUBJECT:
Re-Appropriation to be considered for approval

On September 12, 2022, the Governing Body will be referring a resolution to the Finance
Committee to re-appropriate funds for the Fiscal Year (FY) 2023 City of Cheyenne Budget per
W.S. § 16-4-112 and 16-4-113.  Below is more detailed information regarding this re-
appropriation request.

ENCUMBRANCES AND ROLLOVER ACCOUNTS - RESERVES
Each September my department prepares a large re-appropriation request that includes carryover
balances and encumbrances from the previous fiscal year for various City funds including the 5th
penny, 6th penny, and others.
When reviewing the current year’s budget, you may have noticed that several city funds were not
fully budgeted.  For example, the approved FY 2023 budget for the 6th penny fund only included
$504,995 in payroll and other general operating expenses for the Botanic Gardens and Greenway
Maintenance, but no project costs.  This is because I must wait until late August after everything
for the previous fiscal year has been posted before I can determine what funds are available to
budget for the various 6th penny projects.  In this re-appropriation I have included $16,633,721 in
carryover balances for the 6th penny fund.  This also applies to other funds such as the One Percent
Sales Tax (5th penny) and several grant funds.
Additionally, I am including in this re-appropriation request more detailed information on the
purchase order (PO) encumbrances (see attached report).  A total of $119,990 in General Fund
encumbrances and $6,457,940 in encumbrances for other funds are included.  Encumbrances are
simply purchase orders that were created for goods and services ordered prior to June 30, 2022,
but for many reasons were not received and paid for by the end of last fiscal year.
I have also included detail on two accounts in the General Fund that will be “rolled over”. The
total that was not expended last fiscal year for these two accounts is $43,965.  This includes Parks
and Recreation Projects in the amount of $2,961 and Belvoir Ranch Planning/Development in the
amount of $41,004.
```

### Page 2 of 10

```text
GENERAL FUND – ADDITIONAL REVENUE AND RESERVES
In addition to the encumbrances and rollovers, there are three General Fund requests included for
your consideration:
1. Youth Alternatives Clinical Supervisor Payroll Expenses.   Jay Sullivan is requesting
an increase in payroll expenses for the Youth Alternatives Clinical Supervisor position.
The current Clinical Supervisor will be retiring in February of 2023 and this would allow
for her vacation accrual payout as well as for hiring her replacement for three months to
allow for cross training.  The additional amount requested is $29,531, which will be paid
from reserves.

2. City Engineer’s Professional Services Increase for ADA Transition Plan.   Tom Cobb
is requesting an increase in the City Engineer’s professional services line item for an
Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) Transition Plan in the amount of $70,000, which
will be paid for from reserves.  The intent of the plan is to separate from other existing City
ADA Transition Plans and specifically address accessibility for persons with disabilities
within the City’s public rights-of-way.

3. Miscellaneous Department – Transfer to the Civic Center.   Jason Sanchez is requesting
$314,611 from General Fund reserves for an additional transfer to pay for the hiring of a
Box Office Manager position and cover the Civic Center cash deficit.  Community
Recreation and Events (CRE) reverted $418,267 to General Fund reserves on June 30,
2022, as the department did not spend all of their budget.  Additionally, CRE requested a
higher General Fund subsidy during the FY 2023 budget process that was not funded.
Please see attached memo from Mr. Sanchez for further information.

OTHER FUNDS - ADDITIONAL REVENUE/RESERVES
In addition to carrying over numerous existing grants, the City of Cheyenne has received the
following new grants and donations.  They are as follows, by fund:
Law Enforcement Grant Fund (024) – grants received
• $7,000 – FY 2022 Enforcing Underage Drinking Laws (EUDL)
Federal Grants Fund (025) – grants received
• $618,400 – FY 2022 Economic Development Administration (EDA) Grant – Cheyenne
Railcar Renovation Project
```

